  <h2 id="detailed-answers-h">Umrah Price Questions — Answered in Detail</h2>
  <p>Every question Pakistani pilgrims actually type into Google, answered straight — with real 2026 numbers, no brochure-speak.</p>

  <h3 id="q-20-days">How much does a 20 days Umrah package cost?</h3>
  <p>Twenty days is a less common brochure length than 14 or 21, but operators do sell it — usually as a 12-nights-Makkah + 7-nights-Madinah split. Economy group packages for 20–21 days run <strong>PKR 207,000–265,000</strong> per person on quad sharing, while luxury 5-star versions climb toward <strong>PKR 389,000+</strong>. The per-day math actually favors longer stays: the fixed costs (visa, flights) get spread thinner, which is why a 21-day trip doesn't cost 50% more than a 14-day one. These longer packages suit retirees, Ramadan pilgrims who want the month's feel without peak-week prices, and anyone planning multiple Umrahs via Masjid al-Taneem. If an agent quotes far above PKR 265,000 for an economy 20-day, ask exactly which hotels and what distances — at that length, a shuttle-only hotel gets tiring fast.</p>

  <h3 id="q-7-days">How much does a 7 days Umrah package cost?</h3>
  <p>All-inclusive economy 7-day packages run <strong>PKR 185,000–245,000</strong> per person in 2026. If you already have flights sorted, package-only options (visa + hotels + transport) start around <strong>PKR 89,000</strong>. A typical 7-day split is 4 nights in Makkah and 2 in Madinah — enough for one Umrah, prayers in both Haramain, and a quick ziarat, but honestly rushed for a first-timer. This length is built for working professionals who can't take long leave. One warning: because flights and visa are fixed costs, the 7-day package saves less than you'd expect over a 10-day one — price both before deciding.</p>

  <h3 id="q-10-days">How much is a 10 day Umrah package?</h3>
  <p><strong>PKR 210,000–250,000</strong> for standard economy, and <strong>PKR 345,000–355,000</strong> if you want 5-star hotels. Ten days usually means 6 nights in Makkah and 3–4 in Madinah, which is why Pakistani families call it the sweet spot: you get Jumuah in Masjid al-Haram, a proper Makkah ziarat, and a pace that doesn't exhaust elderly parents — for only a little more than the 7-day price. Most returning pilgrims will tell you the same thing: <em>"I wish I'd booked 10 days instead of 7."</em></p>

  <h3 id="q-total-cost-2026">What is the total cost of an Umrah package from Pakistan in 2026?</h3>
  <p>The honest range is wide: about <strong>PKR 185,000</strong> at the rock-bottom end (7-day economy, quad sharing, off-season) to <strong>PKR 650,000+</strong> for 5-star VIP packages. Most Pakistani families actually pay somewhere between <strong>PKR 245,000 and PKR 410,000</strong> per person for a 10–14 day standard package. Three things drive almost all the variation: your flights (city + season), how close your hotel is to the Haram, and how long you stay. Everything else — ziarat, transport, guides — is small change by comparison. Use the calculator at the top of this page to pin down your own number instead of guessing from a brochure.</p>

  <h3 id="q-cheapest-month">What is the cheapest month for Umrah?</h3>
  <p>Right after Hajj season ends — roughly <strong>Muharram and Safar</strong> — and the <strong>late-summer stretch of June through August</strong>. In those windows, airlines drop fares and hotels near the Haram discount aggressively because the crowds thin out. The flip side: summer in Makkah crosses 45°C, which is genuinely punishing for the elderly, and Muharram/Safar packages sell out fast because every bargain-hunter knows the trick. If you can handle heat and book early, summer is the cheapest Umrah you'll ever do — about 5% below normal months before you even start negotiating.</p>

  <h3 id="q-10-days-pakistan">What is the cost of a 10-day Umrah package from Pakistan?</h3>
  <p>From Pakistan, all-inclusive 10-day economy packages sit at <strong>PKR 210,000–250,000</strong> per person in 2026, with 5-star versions at <strong>PKR 345,000–355,000</strong>. The "from Pakistan" part matters: a Karachi departure can run PKR 15,000–20,000 cheaper than the same package from Peshawar or Quetta, purely on airfare. Always check whether the quoted price includes return flights — some agents advertise a tempting number and add "ticket alag se" later. The calculator above includes flights from your city, so its number is the one to compare quotes against.</p>

  <h3 id="q-14-days-pakistan">How much does a 14-day Umrah package cost from Pakistan?</h3>
  <p>The 14-day package is Pakistan's most-booked Umrah length, and 2026 prices cluster tightly: <strong>PKR 245,000–255,000</strong> for budget 3-star, <strong>PKR 265,000–289,000</strong> for walking-distance saver packages, <strong>PKR 305,000–410,000</strong> for standard 4-star, and <strong>PKR 598,000–720,000</strong> for luxury 5-star. The standard split is 9 nights in Makkah and 5 in Madinah — two full weeks, two Jumuah prayers, unhurried ziarat in both cities. If this is your first Umrah and the budget allows, 14 days is the length experienced pilgrims recommend most.</p>

  <h3 id="q-visa-fee">How much is the Saudi Umrah visa fee for Pakistani?</h3>
  <p>The Saudi government's base visa fee is <strong>SAR 300</strong> — about PKR 22,300 at current rates. But that's not what you'll pay. Pakistanis can't apply directly through the Nusuk app, so the visa comes bundled through a licensed agent with mandatory medical insurance and ground-transport linkage, and the full processing costs <strong>PKR 48,000–80,000</strong> in practice. Anyone quoting you "just the SAR 300" is quoting a fee you can't access on your own. Budget PKR 55,000 for the visa line in your planning — that's the realistic middle.</p>

  <h3 id="q-rupees-required">How many Pakistani rupees are required for Umrah?</h3>
  <p>Minimum realistic budget: around <strong>PKR 185,000 per person</strong> for a 7-day economy package on quad sharing, booked off-season. A comfortable mid-range 14-day trip runs <strong>PKR 280,000–400,000 per person</strong>. For a family of four, plan on <strong>PKR 10–14 lakh</strong> all-in for a standard package — and keep another SAR 1,500–2,500 per person (roughly PKR 110,000–185,000) as pocket money for food, shopping, and extras, since most economy packages don't include meals. Whatever number you land on, add a 10% buffer; the riyal has a habit of moving against the rupee right before you travel.</p>

  <h3 id="q-best-agency">Which travel agency in Pakistan is best for Umrah?</h3>
  <p>No honest website can crown one "best" agency — the right answer depends on your city, budget, and dates. What matters is the checklist: a valid <strong>Ministry of Religious Affairs license</strong> (verify it, don't take their word), a written itemized quotation naming actual hotels and their distances from the Haram, and proper receipts for every payment. Well-known IATA-authorized operators running customized Umrah itineraries from Pakistan include <strong>Azam Travel &amp; Tours, Trips.pk, Sastaticket.pk, and OS Consultants</strong> — but treat even big names as a starting point, not a verdict. Get three written quotes, compare hotel-for-hotel, and walk away from anyone pressuring you into a "today only" advance.</p>

  <h3 id="q-pia-ticket">What is the price of a Pia Umrah ticket in 2026?</h3>
  <p>Round-trip PIA economy tickets for Umrah start around <strong>PKR 141,500 from Karachi</strong> and <strong>PKR 155,500 from Lahore or Islamabad</strong>, before seasonal swings. Ramadan and December seats climb far above that; summer seats can dip below. PIA and Saudia fly direct; Airblue, Serene Air, and Fly Jinnah add competition on some routes. Two practical notes: Umrah fares are bucketed for pilgrim traffic, so they don't move exactly like regular fares — and booking 2–3 months out beats every "deal" you'll find last-minute. If PIA's schedule doesn't suit you, price Saudia on the same dates before assuming PIA is cheapest.</p>

  <h3 id="q-per-person">How much is Umrah per person?</h3>
  <p>Per person, Umrah from Pakistan costs roughly <strong>PKR 185,000–650,000+</strong> in 2026 — but that range is useless without context, so here's the useful version: most people pay <strong>PKR 250,000–350,000 per person</strong> for a 14-day standard package on quad sharing. "Per person" assumes you're splitting rooms: quad sharing divides each room four ways (cheapest), triple costs ~25% more per person, double ~70% more. A couple booking a double room for privacy will pay far more "per person" than four friends in a quad — same hotels, same flights, different division.</p>

  <h3 id="q-7-day-visa-cost">How much does a 7-day Umrah visa cost?</h3>
  <p>Here's what most people don't realize: <strong>the visa costs the same whether you stay 7 days or 28</strong>. The SAR 300 government fee and the PKR 48,000–80,000 agent processing charge don't change with trip length — you're buying entry and insurance, not nights. So a 7-day package doesn't save a rupee on the visa versus a 14-day one; the savings come purely from fewer hotel nights. If an agent tries to charge you a "short-stay visa discount" or a "long-stay visa surcharge," that's invented — the visa fee is flat.</p>

  <h3 id="q-visa-without-agent">How to get Umrah visa from Pakistan without agent?</h3>
  <p>For standard Pakistani passport holders, you effectively can't — and anyone telling you otherwise is selling something. Pakistan isn't on Saudi Arabia's direct tourist eVisa / Nusuk self-application list, so the Umrah visa must be processed through a <strong>licensed Pakistani operator</strong> who sponsors it and links it to your transport and accommodation. The one real exception: if you're flying <strong>Saudia or Flynas</strong> with a layover in the Kingdom, you can get a <strong>free 96-hour transit stopover visa</strong> and perform Umrah on it — but that's 4 days max, not a full package. The practical move isn't dodging agents; it's picking a licensed one and making them earn their fee with a written, itemized quote.</p>

  <h3 id="q-visa-approval-days">How many days take for Umrah visa approval?</h3>
  <p>Standard processing takes <strong>24 to 72 hours</strong> once your licensed agent submits complete documents. "Complete" is doing heavy lifting there — a passport scan with the wrong background or an expired photo restarts the clock. Apply at least two weeks before departure anyway: Ramadan and December–January create backlogs, and you don't want your flights booked while your visa is "in process." If an agent promises a "same-day guaranteed visa," ask what happens to your money if it isn't.</p>

  <h3 id="q-21-days-price">What is the price of a 21 days Umrah package from Pakistan in 2026?</h3>
  <p>Economy 21-day packages run <strong>PKR 207,000–310,000</strong> per person on quad sharing; luxury 5-star versions reach <strong>PKR 389,300+</strong>. The usual split is 13 nights in Makkah and 7 in Madinah — an extended stay built for retirees, Ramadan pilgrims, and anyone who suspects this might be their only Umrah. Per-day, it's the best value of any standard length, because the fixed visa and flight costs spread across three weeks. Just be honest about stamina: three weeks is wonderful at 40 and grueling at 75 — pick your hotel distance accordingly.</p>

  <h3 id="q-umrah-open-2026">Which date will Umrah open in 2026?</h3>
  <p>Umrah doesn't really "open and close" like a season — it's available year-round except during the Hajj period. What actually happens: Saudi Arabia pauses Umrah visa issuance from around <strong>mid-Dhul-Qadah until after Hajj</strong> (Hajj 2026 falls around late May), so new Umrah pilgrims can't enter during those weeks. Once Hajj concludes in <strong>early June 2026</strong>, Umrah visas resume — that's the "opening" people ask about, and it's also when the cheapest post-Hajj packages appear. If you're planning around this, target departures from mid-June onward for the lowest prices of the year.</p>

  <h3 id="q-best-month">Which month is best to do Umrah?</h3>
  <p>It depends what you're optimizing for. <strong>Best weather:</strong> November through February, when Makkah sits in the pleasant 25–35°C range — but you'll pay peak-season prices, especially December–January. <strong>Best value:</strong> June–August or right after Hajj, if you can handle serious heat. <strong>Best spiritual atmosphere:</strong> Ramadan, particularly the last ten nights — at the highest prices of the year. The balanced answer most Pakistani families land on: <strong>November or February</strong>. Good weather, moderate crowds, sane prices — and hotels near the Haram are actually available without booking half a year ahead.</p>

  <h3 id="q-7-steps">What are the 7 steps of Umrah?</h3>
  <p>The rituals of Umrah, in order: <strong>(1)</strong> entering <strong>Ihram</strong> — the state of purity and intent, with the talbiyah on your lips; <strong>(2) Tawaf</strong> — circling the Kaaba seven times counterclockwise; <strong>(3)</strong> praying two rakaat at <strong>Maqam Ibrahim</strong>; <strong>(4)</strong> drinking <strong>Zamzam</strong> water; <strong>(5) Sa'i</strong> — walking seven times between the hills of Safa and Marwa; <strong>(6) Halq or Taqsir</strong> — shaving the head or clipping the hair, which releases you from Ihram; <strong>(7)</strong> respectfully leaving the state of Ihram. The whole sequence takes most first-timers 3–5 hours at a calm pace. Your package's guide usually walks the group through it once — after that, you're on your own, which is exactly as it should be.</p>

  <h3 id="q-visa-online">Can I apply for an Umrah visa online?</h3>
  <p>Yes and no. You can absolutely start the process online — licensed agents take documents over WhatsApp and their websites, and you'll fill forms digitally. But you <strong>cannot complete it yourself end-to-end</strong> the way you'd apply for, say, a UAE visa: Pakistanis aren't on the Nusuk direct-application list, so a licensed operator must sponsor and submit your Umrah visa. What "online" really means here is convenience, not independence. Have ready: a passport valid 6+ months, recent photographs, and your CNIC copy. The agent handles the rest.</p>

  <h3 id="q-evisa-cost">How much is an eVisa for Umrah?</h3>
  <p>The eVisa itself carries the <strong>SAR 300</strong> Saudi government fee (about PKR 22,300), but no Pakistani pays just that. Because the application must route through a licensed operator with mandatory insurance and transport linkage, the all-in eVisa processing lands at <strong>PKR 48,000–80,000</strong>. Don't confuse this with Saudi's tourist eVisa either — that's a different product, and Pakistan isn't on its direct-issue list. When an agent quotes you, ask for the visa+insurance line separated from the package — it's the easiest line to hide padding in.</p>

  <h3 id="q-free-umrah">How to apply for free Umrah by Saudi government?</h3>
  <p>There is <strong>no general "free Umrah" program</strong> to apply for — any website or agent promising one is running a scam, full stop. The single real exception: Saudi Arabia's <strong>96-hour transit stopover visa</strong>, free for passengers transiting on Saudia or Flynas, which does allow you to perform Umrah during the layover. That's four days, not a package, and you still pay for your flights and hotels. Charitable sponsored Umrah trips do exist through mosques and welfare organizations, but those are private initiatives with their own selection — not a government application portal.</p>

  <h3 id="q-pakistani-passport">Can I get an Umrah visa with my Pakistani passport?</h3>
  <p>Yes — hundreds of thousands of Pakistanis do it every year. The Pakistani passport is <strong>fully eligible for Umrah</strong>; you simply apply through a licensed Pakistani operator rather than directly. Standard requirements: passport valid at least 6 months beyond travel, recent white-background photographs, CNIC copy, and a confirmed package (flights + hotels + transport). Processing is 24–72 hours once documents are complete. The passport isn't the obstacle — picking a trustworthy agent is.</p>

